Success Criteria — Operating Model Program Maximization (Descriptive Version)

This year, I focused on strengthening the operating model by improving the quality of the data that flows into our outreach programs. A big part of my work was making sure the member lists we delivered were clean, accurate, and ready for action—without the noise of unreachable, inactive, or invalid contacts. I spent time understanding the business rules, aligning the filters with program needs, and validating that each list truly represented the right members to focus on.

My work with EPMP played a key role in this. By identifying DNC/DNE members and filtering them out early, I helped ensure that operational teams spent their time only on members who could actually be contacted. This not only reduced wasted effort but also made the outreach process more compliant and more effective. I also paid close attention to the details—verifying contact readiness, checking eligibility logic, and making sure every dataset we produced was reliable and straightforward for the teams to use.

Through this approach, I was able to reduce the amount of bad data going into the programs and improve the precision of our member targeting. The end result was smoother operations, more efficient resource use, and higher productivity across the outreach teams. Contributing to this part of the operating model has been meaningful, and I’ve seen firsthand how the improvements in data quality directly support better outcomes for the business.



Success Criteria — Evolve Our Multi-Payer Capabilities and Technology for Quality Stars (Descriptive Version)

A major part of my work this year involved making sure that multi-payer members were treated with the same level of importance and accuracy as our UHC members. I took the time to understand how each payer defines its quality goals, what measures matter to them, and how our data workflows need to adapt so we can support those requirements. Instead of building processes only around UHC datasets, I made sure that multi-payer members were fully included in every step—whether it was outreach readiness, targeting logic, or gaps-in-care identification.

From a data perspective, I focused on improving how we identify multi-payer members and how we prepare their data so it’s clean, complete, and aligned with each payer’s Stars and quality expectations. This meant validating eligibility, double-checking contact information, and confirming that the right members were being flagged for outreach. By doing this, we made sure we weren’t unintentionally excluding multi-payer members or missing opportunities to close their open measures.

The work directly contributed to stronger multi-payer performance. With more accurate data, operational teams had the confidence to reach out to these members, close their gaps, and support the quality goals for each payer. In the end, we built processes that didn’t just support one payer—we elevated our capabilities to support all payers consistently, helping strengthen our overall Stars outcomes across the board.
Success Criteria — Drive to 80% in 4+ Stars Through Optimal Business Enablement (Descriptive Version)

One of the biggest areas where I contributed this year was in strengthening the quality of the data that feeds our outreach programs. A lot of the Stars performance depends on whether we are reaching the right members, and that starts with having clean, accurate, and compliant calling lists. I spent significant time validating TCPA data, Gryphon data, and eligibility information to make sure we were not sending operational teams after the wrong members.

I focused heavily on removing litigators, inactive members, and any records that could put the company at risk or lead to wasted outreach. Even filtering out a single layer of inactive members moves us one step closer to an outreach-ready list—and that directly improves our chances of closing more gaps and supporting Stars performance. Instead of letting bad data flow downstream, I made sure our processes stop those issues early.

By improving the quality of the calling lists and ensuring that only the right members are included, we not only reduce compliance exposure but also make the outreach teams more productive. Every clean list we deliver helps drive better performance, higher contact rates, and ultimately contributes to our overall goal of reaching and maintaining 80% in 4+ Stars. My work this year helped create a stronger foundation that the business can rely on to support accurate, efficient, and compliant member engagement.
